WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.220
As of this video, you can now support PolyMatter on Patreon. I'll tell you more at the end of this video, but for now, let's get to it.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:13.240
Black Mirror is a TV show that’s completely
terrifying and also impossible to stop watching…

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.070
But it’s hard to describe why it’s so
good because it’s hard to define what it

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.120
even is.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.130
Each episode is so independent, there aren’t
many common characteristics.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.130
Except one.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.820
Many people see the show as a critique of
technology.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.860
And if that’s the case, it might be fun
to watch, but isn’t all that profound or

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:30.860
new.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.410
Here’s an article making this very point:

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.180
“Black Mirror’s messages are usually pretty
simple.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.180
Cell phones?

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.180
Bad.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.180
Reality shows?

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.180
Bad.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.180
Social media?

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.180
Really bad.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.020
Oh, sure, the setup and the execution of those
ideas is impressive, but the show’s primary

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.660
crutch is too often that it uses thought-provoking
and fascinating foundations in order to reach

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:53.230
the simplest, most alarmist possible conclusion
about a variety of technological innovations.”

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.080
I’ve also heard people describe the writing
as far too unrealistic.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.730
It’s hard to imagine some of these worlds
ever coming true.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.899
But both of these complaints miss the point.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:05.250
Black Mirror isn’t actually about technology
- or the future.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.159
But it’s not just that I enjoy the show,
I also think it’s more meaningful than many

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:10.440
give it credit for.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.240
So what makes it so good, and what is it really
about?

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.549
The enemy always looks like technology because
it’s always present for those signature

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:23.659
jaw-dropping Black Mirror moments, but its
purpose is only to emphasize the characters

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:24.670
and their actions.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.750
In White Christmas, Joe discovers Beth is
pregnant, the two get in a fight, and Beth

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.539
uses her digital eyepiece to permanently block
him in real life.

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:36.899
After years of desperation, Joe catches the
first glance of his daughter and it all becomes

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:41.709
clear: Beth used this feature to hide her
infidelity: it isn’t his child.

00:01:41.709 --> 00:01:45.969
Horrifying as this technology is, it’s only
a vehicle for the thoughts and desires of

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:47.880
the characters themselves.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.279
It isn’t responsible for her infidelity
or willingness to hide it for all those years.

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:56.719
This is a theme throughout the series: - The
people in White Bear want revenge, technology

00:01:56.719 --> 00:01:58.630
is just along for the ride.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.990
- And Nosedive and Fifteen Million Merits
have technology that ruins lives, but it only

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.359
works because of people’s willingness to
comply

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:10.270
Black Mirror uses technology as a writing
device, a tool to expose who we are in a way

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.360
that seems new - but isn’t really the focus.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.830
Each story is really about problems we already
have, in a world without the episode’s technology.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.530
And if you have any doubt about this, here’s
the creator of the show himself:

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:28.810
It’s a show about people, and how the traits
we all have can go very wrong, especially

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.150
when we’re enabled by the world around us.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.379
Which I think explains why some episodes are
much better than others.

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:37.800
Here’s a poll of twenty thousand people’s
favorite episodes.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.219
The higher rated episodes have well developed,
relatable characters, but less focus on sophisticated

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:44.219
technology.

00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:47.340
And the futuristic worlds with more bland
characters rank lower.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.849
I agree most episodes aren’t realistic - but
I consider it a feature not a bug.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:55.360
A good dystopia is a warning, not a prediction.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.870
1984 and Brave New World are overstated, and
that’s what makes them interesting.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.959
The point isn’t to be accurate but to take
very real and important ideas and make them

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:06.720
entertaining enough that we’ll actually
pay attention.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.659
And to test all of this, we can compare two
episodes:

00:03:09.659 --> 00:03:12.500
Structurally, White Bear and Shut Up And Dance
are very similar:

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.829
We’re put in the shoes of a character who
we can relate to - they, like us, are confused

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:17.829
and worried.

00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:21.790
And as they’re put in increasingly crazy
situations, we realize who our characters

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:22.810
really are.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:27.230
We’re horrified, but we’re supposed to
be even more horrified by everyone else.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.709
The excessive retribution and the off-putting
joy it gives them.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.420
They end by showing the scale of the injustice
- how Victoria must endure her torture every

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.200
day, and how many people are being played
like Kenny.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.400
So the stories are similar, but Shut Up and
Dance is ranked considerably higher - why

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.400
is that?

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.579
I think it supports the theory - Shut Up and
Dance has better characters, and that’s

00:03:46.579 --> 00:03:48.379
what the show is really about.

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:53.159
Because the intended message is only communicated
if we feel bad for our character, which makes

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:54.790
us hate their antagonist.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:59.250
We empathize with Victoria in White Bear because
we’re just as confused as she is.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:03.849
But Shut Up and Dance has a longer exposition
- we really get to know Kenny before the adventure

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:06.930
begins - we learn who he is and how he lives.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.980
So we’re more empathetic when he’s in
trouble, and more distressed when he’s exposed.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.270
But none of this wastes any time - those early
scenes eventually come back to haunt us with

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.270
new meaning.

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:19.970
It also has more big reveals, it just keeps
hitting us on the head.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:23.580
When they open the cake and we realize the
seriousness of the situation,

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.350
Then when we meet the real Kenny,

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:30.010
And after all that, when their secrets are
still released in one final, devastating,

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.010
“screw you”.

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:34.690
Which some people dislike - the troll face
can come across as a bit absurd.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.830
I actually think it’s important for developing
that character.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.510
It’s silly and childish - but it gives us
a sense of the person behind it.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.590
Some young, reckless person who does this
for the thrill.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.090
Completely unrestrained by the law or any
sense of human empathy.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:52.200
Black Mirror is good because it’s incredibly
entertaining to watch a character be built

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.160
up and then come crashing down in an instant.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.010
And also because those characters are us.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:03.820
Behind every disaster is a perfectly familiar
human characteristic that you have too, just

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.240
waiting for the wrong situation to become
your downfall.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.610
